#: src/development/release-notes.md:3
msgid "Release Notes"
msgstr ""

#: src/development/release-notes.md:5
msgid ""
"Whenever you open a pull request, the body is automatically populated based "
"on this [pull request template](https://github.com/zed-industries/zed/blob/"
"main/.github/pull_request_template.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/development/release-notes.md:15
msgid ""
"On Wednesdays, we run [`get-preview-channel-changes`](https://github.com/zed-"
"industries/zed/blob/main/script/get-preview-channel-changes), which collects "
"`Release Notes` lines from pull requests landing in preview, as described in "
"the [Release](https://zed.dev/docs/development/release-notes) docs."
msgstr ""

#: src/development/release-notes.md:17
msgid ""
"The script outputs everything below the `Release Notes` line, including "
"metadata such as the pull request author (if they are not a Zed team member) "
"and a link to the pull request. If you use `N/A`, the script skips your pull "
"request entirely."
msgstr ""

#: src/development/release-notes.md:20
msgid "Guidelines for crafting your `Release Notes` line(s)"
msgstr ""

#: src/development/release-notes.md:22
msgid ""
"A `Release Notes` line should only be written if the user can see or feel "
"the difference in Zed."
msgstr ""

#: src/development/release-notes.md:23
msgid ""
"A `Release Notes` line should be written such that a Zed user can understand "
"what the change is. Don't assume a user knows technical editor developer "
"lingo; phrase your change in language they understand as a user of a text "
"editor."
msgstr ""

#: src/development/release-notes.md:25
msgid ""
"If you want to include technical details about your pull request for other "
"team members to see, do so above the `Release Notes` line."
msgstr ""

#: src/development/release-notes.md:26
msgid "Changes to docs should be labeled as `N/A`."
msgstr ""

#: src/development/release-notes.md:27
msgid ""
"If your pull request adds/changes a setting or a keybinding, always mention "
"that setting or keybinding. Don't make the user dig into docs or the pull "
"request to find this information (although it should be included in docs as "
"well)."
msgstr ""

#: src/development/release-notes.md:29
msgid "For pull requests that are reverts:"
msgstr ""

#: src/development/release-notes.md:30
msgid ""
"If the item being reverted **has already been shipped**, include a `Release "
"Notes` line explaining why we reverted, as this is a breaking change."
msgstr ""

#: src/development/release-notes.md:31
msgid ""
"If the item being reverted **hasn't been shipped**, edit the original PR's "
"`Release Notes` line to `N/A`; otherwise, it will still be included and the "
"release notes compiler may not know to skip it."
msgstr ""
